  "summary": "Simulation and benchmark studies now test whether sparse, multi-source aDNA designs preserve kinship and selection inference validity.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that sampling design, rather than estimator choice alone, determines whether population-genetic inference is valid. A simulation study of sedimentary aDNA finds that multi-contributor samples impair kinship inference and proposes a heterozygosity test to flag them. A benchmark of four allele-frequency time-series selection methods (ApproxWF, BMWS, Slattice, Sr) on simulated aDNA-like sparse data assesses their power under these conditions. In domestication genomics, a modified STRUCTURE parameterization reduces sampling and Ne biases in the walnut analysis, supporting a South Asian origin. The amphioxus mutation-rate study addresses a different question and is adjacent only. What is new is treating sparse and mixed-source sampling as a determinant of inference validity rather than background noise.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-02-03"
}
